    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_cf_211'), 'int a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int *end = a + 4;

void f(int a[4]);   /* defined elsewhere */

Which statements are true on x86-64 Linux with gcc? (Select all that apply.)', 'multiple_choice'::question_type, 'a + 4 is the one-past-the-end pointer: forming it and comparing with it (end != a) is valid C, and end - a is 4 elements of type ptrdiff_t. Reading *end is undefined behaviour, because there is no element there; ASan reports it as a stack-buffer-overflow. Inside f, the parameter int a[4] is adjusted to int *a, so sizeof a is the size of a pointer (8 here), not 16; gcc even warns with -Wsizeof-array-argument. const int *q = a; makes the ints read-only through q, but q itself can still move, so q++ compiles. a + 5 is not even allowed to be computed: only pointers into the array and one past its end are valid.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_cf_211') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
